// Working decisions for udon v2, kept by hand. Newest last; a later entry that
// overturns an earlier one says which.
//
// Everything here so far is a LIGHT WORKING LEAN: cheap to reconsider, and
// recorded so that a later mind does not read it back as a ruling. `how` says
// what was actually done in deciding, as the least the words support:
//   proposed  - put forward by the named party; nobody else has taken it up
//   lean      - the steward's current inclination, in his words
//   expects   - the steward's prediction about where the work will go; not a choice
//   possible  - named as an option, expressly undecided
//   assented  - put to the steward by someone else, and he agreed
//   stated    - the steward said how something already decided is meant to work
// `words` are the steward's own, from the session of 2026-11-20, with an
// obvious misspelling replaced by the whole corrected word in [brackets].
// `holds` is the recording agent's reading of them, and is the weaker of the two.
// `by` and `at` are typed by the recording agent, not read from a harness.
// KDL 2.0. A value containing " is a raw string, #"like this"#.

decision "bootstrap-from-markdown-and-kdl" how="lean" by="Joseph" at="2026-11-20" {
  holds "Until udon can be pinned and used for its own spec, segments are markdown and KDL; the mix is moved into udon as soon as udon is far enough along."
  words "the plan is for us to transition the markdown/KDL mix into udon as soon as udon is far enough along to pin and bootstrap it."
}

decision "segment-formats" how="proposed" by="Joseph" at="2026-11-20" {
  holds "Prose-primary segments are markdown; structure-heavy segments are KDL; a markdown segment's frontmatter is a leading ```kdl block, not --- YAML."
  words #"I'd like to propose that we use markdown for segments that are prose-primary for now in udon (pre-bootstrap), and kdl for structure-heavy segments as well as "```kdl" first lines in the markdowns instead of '---' yaml for frontmatter blocks (so, like we have in ~/src/verisectorium/core/def/*.md )."#
}

decision "term-notation" how="proposed" by="Joseph" at="2026-11-20" {
  holds "Spec-defined terms are written as verisectorium core writes its terms: «term», ‹subtype›."
  words "Also that we go with that same core's notation for spec-defined terms"
}

decision "one-term-per-file" how="lean" by="Joseph" at="2026-11-20" {
  holds "A term definition is one term in one file; terms defined against each other name each other with a dual field. Whether this is a default or a restriction is not settled."
  words "a 1:1 between term:file should be the default-- or even restriction-- dual-terms etc. can just depend on each other in their dual field or something."
}

decision "definitions-first" how="lean" by="Joseph" at="2026-11-20" {
  holds "The spec's term definitions are worked first, before the rules that use them."
  words "One thing we'll improve on significantly over spec-lite-0.1.0 is we will focus initially on the lexicon / term definitions which were, in the spec-lite project, left as afterthoughts-- causing enormous churn and confusion and exponentially exploding jargon-size."
}

decision "canonical-outline-reader" how="lean" by="Joseph" at="2026-11-20" {
  holds "spec/canonical.outline.md is for someone implementing a compliant parser whose output, for fully parsed input, is an AST of the shape the spec gives."
  words "It will be written as if to someone implementing a compliant parser that outputs (in non-streaming contexts / fully parsed chunks) a [compliantly] shaped AST."
}

decision "ast-with-the-spec" how="expects" by="Joseph" at="2026-11-20" {
  holds "The non-streaming AST is specified together with the spec, as what the spec is tested against."
  words "I think that the non-streaming AST representation will be hand in hand with the spec to give the spec concrete reality to test against."
}

decision "pedagogy-outline" how="expects" by="Joseph" at="2026-11-20" {
  holds "A pedagogy outline comes later, over the same segments plus its own."
  words "It is likely that pedagogy will end up being a superset that also pulls in some spec segments as appropriate and depends on most/all of them."
}

decision "grammar-placement" how="expects" by="Joseph" at="2026-11-20" {
  holds "No separate grammar outline yet; grammar begins as sections and segments inside the spec."
  words "Grammar, however, is more difficult for us I think to say this early-- it might start evolving -- at least at first, as sections and segments within spec that are PEG representations of parts of the stuff, or railway diagrams etc."
}

decision "fixtures-from-segments" how="expects" by="Joseph" at="2026-11-20" {
  holds "Exemplar, idiom, edge-case and avoid segments, and fixtures, sit among the spec segments they depend on and are assembled into the test fixtures."
  words "I suspect we'll have exemplars/idioms/edge-cases/avoid/ etc. + thorough fixtures that will all be assembled into actual testing fixtures as well-- sprinkled throughout and depending on parts of the spec."
}

decision "spec-lite-is-the-source" how="lean" by="Joseph" at="2026-11-20" {
  holds "Segments, and the other segment kinds to adopt, are pulled first from .archive/v2/spec-lite-0.1.0/."
  words "the *actual* segments we'll start pulling from are in .archive/v2/spec-lite-0.1.0/"
}

decision "address-theory-defs-in-influx" how="lean" by="Joseph" at="2026-11-20" {
  holds "address-theory's definitions are kept as a copy in spec/.influx/at-defs/ and placed one at a time as the spec needs them."
  words "They at least belong, as a copy, in spec/.influx/at-defs/ or something and we can adjudicate them and add them as needed etc."
}

decision "outline-columns" how="proposed" by="Claude Fable 5.2 session" at="2026-11-20" {
  holds "The canonical outline starts with a slot reference, a kind and a one-line claim, and gains a standing column only when something computes it."
  words #"Joseph, of that proposal: "Agreed as far as what I feel is minimal... mostly. I think verisectorium/core/ outline(s) have what it is more likely to evolve into." And: "It wouldn't surprise me if individual RFC 2119 "requirement levels" ends up being a column.""#
}

decision "version-is-the-spec's" how="lean" by="Joseph" at="2026-11-20" {
  holds "The VERSION file at the root versions the spec only, for now."
  words "initially we're just versioning the spec, not the parser or tooling."
}

decision "project-tooling-in-ruby-under-tools" how="lean" by="Joseph" at="2026-11-20" {
  holds "Project-level scripts are Ruby, written as dx tools in tools/; bin/ is not used for now."
  words #"I'm actually leaning toward Ruby again for "project-level" work / one-off scripts etc. If devex/dx is ready as it is, let's skip bin/ for now and just write what we need in tools/ (including a spec-bump command or something)."#
}

decision "vsect-alpha-pilot" how="possible" by="Joseph" at="2026-11-20" {
  holds "Assembly and linting of the spec might be done by a vsect-alpha tool developed in verisectorium and piloted here."
  words "we could, if we decide, *actually* pilot vsect-alpha, developing it over there and using it here for the assembly of the spec etc."
}

decision "spec-version-form" how="assented" by="Joseph" at="2026-11-20" {
  holds #"The spec's version is written in SemVer 2.0 form, MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH-stage.N: a hyphen, the stage, a dot, a counter (2.0.0-alpha.1). It is the spec's version only. A Rust implementation does not exist yet; when it does it has a version of its own, and nothing here says what that will be. Stages, with the meanings the 0.9 line gave them: alpha (evolving), beta (feature-complete), rc (frozen, being validated), then the bare version. N goes up each time a resting state of the canonical outline is delivered."#
  put-by "Claude Fable 5.2 session, as its lean among the prerelease conventions it laid out"
  words "I agree with your lean. We just need to be clear somewhere that we're adopting the semver form for the spec's version-- distinct from (the currently non-existing) rust-implementation's version."
  open "Whether VERSION names the last delivered state or the one being worked toward. Recorded for now as the former: 2.0.0-alpha.0 until a first resting state is delivered. Not put to Joseph yet."
}

decision "spec-version-file" how="lean" by="Joseph" at="2026-11-20" {
  holds "The spec's version lives in VERSION.SPEC at the root, named `spec` in .dx.yml, and is shown and changed with `dx version spec …`. This replaces the file name in version-is-the-spec's; the file name itself was offered loosely."
  words "we'll move VERSION here to VERSION.SPEC or something and add a line and have it `dx version spec bump pre` (with no version subcommand defaulting to VERSION file or whatever its current behavior is)..."
}

decision "term-forms-inside-guillemets" how="stated" by="Joseph" at="2026-11-20" {
  holds "A term is written in guillemets in whichever grammatical form the sentence needs, with the whole form inside: «attribute», «attributes». A term's other forms (plural, possessive where needed) are declared for exactly this. The convention holds in the outline as well as in segments."
  words "It looks like you are using the guillemet convention almost correctly in the element.md -- keep in mind that we specify the other grammatical forms (plural, possessive if necessary, etc.) specifically so we can say «attribute» and «attributes» interchangeably as the sentence's grammar demands."
}

decision "outline-kinds-inline-term-and-full-term" how="lean" by="Joseph" at="2026-11-20" {
  holds "The canonical outline has a kind column. A term defined only by its row is of kind inline-term; a term with its own file is of kind full-term; they are different kinds because different things can be asked of them. More kinds come as rows need them. This replaces outline-columns: the columns are now mark, kind, term, description, with mark entered by hand as in verisectorium core."
  words "kind can be  inline-term vs full-term (vs other things we'll have soon-- but an inline term *is* a different kind, because it has different (mostly much fewer) dimensions)."
}

decision "outline-shows-version-by-transclusion" how="lean" by="Joseph" at="2026-11-20" {
  holds "The outline shows the spec's version by transcluding VERSION.SPEC, not by repeating it."
  words "`Version: ![[../VERSION.SPEC]]` (etc.)"
}

decision "inline-terms-take-guillemets" how="proposed" by="Claude Fable 5.2 session" at="2026-11-20" {
  holds "An inline-term is written in guillemets like a full-term («node», «AST»), which departs from verisectorium core, where inline words are written plainly. Reason: a reader cannot otherwise tell a defined word from the ordinary one, and which kind of term it is shows in the outline's kind column. Applied in the outline and in def/element.md; Joseph has not answered it."
}

decision "inline-terms-take-guillemets-agreed" how="assented" by="Joseph" at="2026-11-20" {
  holds "Terms defined inline in the outline are written in guillemets like terms with their own file. This settles inline-terms-take-guillemets, which was the session's proposal."
  put-by "Claude Fable 5.2 session"
  words "Agreed."
}

decision "single-guillemets-for-meta-terms" how="lean" by="Joseph" at="2026-11-20" {
  holds "Single guillemets mark meta-terms: words of the verisectorium method and of this store's own ontology (column names and values such as ‹draft›, ‹definition›, ‹inline·def›), and the requirement words ‹must›, ‹should›, ‹may›. Double guillemets stay for terms the specification defines about Udon. This takes single guillemets away from subtypes, which term-notation had given them after verisectorium core; subtypes have no notation for now. The meta-terms are not defined yet."
  words #"I also added single-guillemets for meta-defined special definitions like column values (verisectorium stuff) and special markers like RFC 2119 definitions etc., instead of "semi-defined/semi-special" meanings as the old verisectorium core has them."#
  words-2 "We'll probably define them at some point and/or put a legend in here, but no point in doing that before we have any SOPs written and before Verisectorium is settled-- the single-guillemets will help us distinguish them as meta-special in the spec for now regardless."
}

decision "outline-order-is-for-comprehension" how="stated" by="Joseph" at="2026-11-20" {
  holds "Rows may use a term before its own row. Order is chosen for a reader's comprehension, not to avoid forward references."
  words "I removed the zero-forward-reference false constraint, so this is a comprehension question now [as] it should be rather than a pedanticity question."
}

decision "ast-means-ast" how="stated" by="Joseph" at="2026-11-20" {
  holds "AST keeps its ordinary meaning. Parsing a partial gives any number of root nodes, root in the tree sense. A document's AST has exactly one root node: an element named $root, an element like any other, whose child nodes other than its metadata are the AST of the document's partial. No anonymous holder element, and no special sense of AST."
  words "AST means AST, a partial gets parsed into any number of udon root nodes (in the tree sense), but a document's AST has a single root node, which is an element with the name $root, whose (non-meta-data) node children are the AST of the content partial."
  rejected "The session's two proposals before this: that the root is a position and not a named element, and that an AST is a pair of attributes and nodes distinct from a node. His words on the second: \"still too much hoop-jumping in my opinion-- the tail wagging the dog so-to-speak\"."
}

decision "consumer-vs-implementer-projections-and-failure-modes" how="lean" by="Joseph" at="2026-11-23" {
  holds "Consumer vs. Implementer are distinct projections of the specification (clarifying the shared boundary role of the AST and examples), and fitness joins locality, modifiability, and accountability across three failure modes: the spec fails, an implementation fails to comply with the spec, or a compliant parser fails at UDON's primary purposes."
  words #"I felt that the CONSUMER vs IMPLEMENTER as two distinct projections of the spec is clarifying-- especially when it comes to some things that were ambiguous to me still like the importance of the AST and examples etc."#
  words-2 "Then, yeah, he mentions the Locality, modifiability, accountability, and I would add fitness.... which got me thinking-- we really have three potential failure modes here:\n- The spec can fail (especially consistency etc.)\n- The implementation may fail to comply w/ the spec\n- The resulting successfully implemented udon parser against the spec may fail at Udon's primary purposes (which we haven't yet moved in from .influx / .archive )"
}

decision "non-segment-footnote-citations" how="lean" by="Joseph" at="2026-11-23" {
  holds "Non-segment working documents (PRAXIS, CHARTER, etc.) use footnote citations liberally, accompanied by one blanket disclaimer above the footnotes noting that cited file locations will likely move and readers should use git and search to locate them."
  words #"I'm going to recommend that in the non-segment files at the very least (like PRAXES/PRAXIS, CHARTER, etc.) we go ahead and use footnote citations somewhat liberally -- with one blanket disclaimer near the bottom footnote bodies that says "the locations of these files will almost certainly change but they should still be in the repo; please use git and search to find their current location as necessary so we don't have to maintain the relative or absolute locations here." etc. or something like that..."#
}

decision "ocaml-auxiliary-and-staged-toolchain" how="lean" by="Joseph" at="2026-11-23" {
  holds "A tiny, pure OCaml/ML subset is a strong candidate for UDON's executable auxiliary tier (AST types, equality predicates, and compiled examples) and similarly in verisectorium. Longer-term, a three-stage OCaml path (auxiliary model -> readable OCaml reference/streaming simulator as syntax rules arrive -> Rust target emission or oracle-validated Rust parser) looks wise without getting ahead of ourselves; the key technical spike for Stage 2/3 is testing how cleanly OCaml handles UDON's specialized indent/dedent and column-positioning rules (with or without a minimal lexing pass)."
  words "Love it love it-- and I'll bet many areas of verisectorium, even barring nice results from the spikes, will use similar constructs."
  words-2 "I think this is a very wise direction as well. I don't know if we want to get ahead of ourselves quite yet here, but we should mark down all of our thoughts on it so far. The main spike to run on thread 3 here is how elegant ocaml can (or cannot, with or without a minimal theoretical lexing pass) do udon's almost pythonic but specialized indent/dedent and column positioning aspects..."
}
